00:47:16,480 --> 00:47:34,640
And I think in the world of apps, I think most people kind of over index on pricing and think that that's going to be the key thing to figure this out when a lot of times there's actually a pretty big gap between, you know, kind of where you can make money and where your customer is willing to pay.

582
00:47:34,880 --> 00:47:37,280
There's a lot of room, a lot of different pricices.

583
00:47:37,440 --> 00:47:41,280
And as long as you launch somewhere in there, you're going to make some money.

584
00:47:41,600 --> 00:47:47,040
And over time, there's lots of ways to become more sophisticated and get to a better and better price point.

585
00:47:47,680 --> 00:47:59,200
But a lot of people assume that it's a that they have a highly elastic product, meaning that for every dollar you increase your pricing, your number of customers drops by a predictable percentage.

586
00:47:59,520 --> 00:48:02,400
And I think in many cases, a lot of products that are inelastic.

587
00:48:02,560 --> 00:48:05,440
If I use it, I'll pay anywhere between five and ten dollars a month.

588
00:48:05,920 --> 00:48:07,520
And if I don't use it, I'll pay nothing.

589
00:48:08,480 --> 00:48:10,720
And so if you notice that people are not canceling.

590
00:48:11,600 --> 00:48:15,760
and they're the same people who are not using the product, it's probably not a pricing problem.

591
00:48:16,000 --> 00:48:17,120
It's probably a product problem.

592
00:48:17,440 --> 00:48:17,680
Right.

593
00:48:17,760 --> 00:48:24,800
I mean, if you're talking about product market fit and a forever relationship, like, that I'm going to pay infinite money in terms of my lifetime, right?

594
00:48:25,040 --> 00:48:27,200
Like, I'm going to pay money until I die.

595
00:48:27,600 --> 00:48:27,760
Right.

596
00:48:28,400 --> 00:48:28,480
Right.

597
00:48:28,640 --> 00:48:36,720
And the thing is that people assume, like, so what I would say is, if you, if you're trying to figure out your first price, I'd say don't worry about it too much.

598
00:48:37,920 --> 00:48:47,120
If you need to do a land grab like Spotify, price low and you can raise your price later, although that's hard, but just do it because you want people to adopt your solution.

599
00:48:48,400 --> 00:48:59,760
If you're worried about hurting your core business, and so then start by pricing really high and you can lower it as you have increased confidence and understanding of use case, but there's a lot of room in there.

600
00:49:00,320 --> 00:49:03,920
And that's really my advice is be somewhere in that range.

601
00:49:05,120 --> 00:49:12,240
And if people aren't buying it or aren't staying, look for the other signs of what might be driving it besides pricing.

602
00:49:12,400 --> 00:49:19,440
Like is it that they, you know, failure to launch, they never onboarded, they never activated, they never used the, you know, best features.

603
00:49:20,240 --> 00:49:23,680
Um, is it that they were using it for a while and then their usage trickled off?

604
00:49:23,920 --> 00:49:25,600
Maybe they used it up, right?

605
00:49:25,920 --> 00:49:28,640
Either they binged or, you know, they've watched everything they've seen.

606
00:49:28,880 --> 00:49:29,920
Maybe their job changed.

607
00:49:30,160 --> 00:49:35,840
So these features are no longer relevant to their work, but really try to be a detective about where the problem is.

608
00:49:36,000 --> 00:49:38,720
Like I sometimes say, it's like you have a party in a bar.

609
00:49:39,120 --> 00:49:40,800
You're not making money from the party in the bar.

610
00:49:41,280 --> 00:49:47,200
Like before you lower the price at the front door, see like are people walking by and not recognizing that you have a party.

611
00:49:47,360 --> 00:49:48,320
So you have nobody in there?

612
00:49:48,720 --> 00:49:49,920
Because that's a awareness problem.

613
00:49:50,160 --> 00:49:52,560
Or is it that people come in the front door and.

614
00:49:52,720 --> 00:49:55,200
And they can't find their way to the food and drink and music.

615
00:49:55,360 --> 00:49:56,640
And so they think it's a lame party.

616
00:49:56,880 --> 00:49:58,560
And so they leave and they never come back.

617
00:49:59,200 --> 00:50:00,640
You know, that's an onboarding problem.

618
00:50:01,200 --> 00:50:05,760
Is it that they've been eating all the food and dancing to all the music and they're like, I'm tired of these songs.

619
00:50:05,840 --> 00:50:09,840
I'm tired of this food, which is a different kind of product problem, product assortment problem.

620
00:50:10,480 --> 00:50:17,520
Or is it I went downstairs to the food and there was no food and the music, you know, the speakers were not working and that's an operational issue.

621
00:50:17,920 --> 00:50:18,080
Right?

622
00:50:18,320 --> 00:50:20,560
So fix the problems before you drop the price.

623
00:50:21,040 --> 00:50:22,080
That's such great advice.

624
00:50:22,400 --> 00:50:23,200
I mean, I think about it.

625
00:50:23,920 --> 00:50:25,840
Sorry, I'm going to extend our podcast one minute.

626
00:50:26,480 --> 00:50:27,920
Just to concur with the product.

627
00:50:28,240 --> 00:50:30,320
If you have product market fit, you're going to go this way.

628
00:50:30,480 --> 00:50:33,600
And I'm just gesticulating on a podcast, never good up and to the right.

629
00:50:33,840 --> 00:50:34,000
Right.

630
00:50:34,320 --> 00:50:37,840
All the price is going to do is maybe define that inflection on that curve, whatever.

631
00:50:37,920 --> 00:50:42,080
But exponential curves like the slope doesn't often all that much in the long term.

632
00:50:42,320 --> 00:50:42,480
Right.

633
00:50:42,640 --> 00:50:48,080
You can optimize it eventually, but it's really getting that product market fit and then it kind of just takes care of itself.

634
00:50:48,240 --> 00:50:48,960
So sorry.

635
00:50:49,120 --> 00:50:50,480
I will let us end the podcast now.

636
00:50:52,320 --> 00:50:55,440
Well, that is a great bit of advice to wrap up on.

637
00:50:55,920 --> 00:51:00,640
And your book, Forever Transaction, was fantastic and reading it was.

638
00:51:01,200 --> 00:51:21,840
so fun just to think about, you know, we put our kind of blinders on with this podcast and in the space we work in with apps, but realizing that so many of the ideas that we think about, so many of the problems we work on are things that are across the entire industry, across all consumer subscriptions, even a lot of overlapping in B2B SaaS.

639
00:51:22,640 --> 00:51:26,560
So it was just so fun reading your book and then getting to ask you questions here.

640
00:51:27,040 --> 00:51:29,760
I had like thirty more questions that I wanted to ask you.

641
00:51:30,240 --> 00:51:31,680
We could do another hour or two.

642
00:51:32,480 --> 00:51:38,880
But I'll put links to your LinkedIn, to your website, to your Twitter in the show notes.

643
00:51:39,520 --> 00:51:42,640
But is there anything else you wanted to share with our audience as we wrap up?

644
00:51:43,200 --> 00:51:44,880
No, I think we covered a lot.

645
00:51:45,040 --> 00:52:06,240
I mean, if there's one thing that I want to leave people with, it's this idea that if you start with the promise you're making to your customers, helping them with an ongoing problem or achieving a ongoing goal that's important to them, and then you optimize your offering around that, your chances of both acquiring and retaining your customers are going to go way up.

646
00:52:06,720 --> 00:52:07,520
Such great advice.

